- How would you adapt it if dictionary lookups are expensive and you need pruning?
- How would you extend to handle negative scores or required minimum total?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests stability, memory constraints, and practical trade-offs between sorting algorithms. It asks for a concrete approach rather than a generic answer.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sorting
- Bottom-up merge sort
- Time/space complexity: O(n log n) time, O(n) extra space

## Code Example

```javascript
function stableSort(arr){
  const n = arr.length; 
  const aux = new Array(n);
  for (let width = 1; width < n; width *= 2){
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i += width * 2){
      const left = i;
      const mid = Math.min(i + width, n);
      const right = Math.min(i + width * 2, n);
      let p = left, q = mid, k = left;
      while (p < mid && q < right){
        if (arr[p].score <= arr[q].score) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
        else aux[k++] = arr[q++];
      }
      while (p < mid) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
      while (q < right) aux[k++] = arr[q++];
      for (let t = left; t < right; t++) arr[t] = aux[t];
    }
  }
  return arr;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for memory-constrained environments where n is very large?
- Compare stability guarantees and real-time performance between mergesort and quicksort in streaming data.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over partitioned sequences with a non-additive batch cost and compares memoization vs tabulation. It also probes edge-case reasoning when K bounds vary.

## Key Concepts
- DP over contiguous partitions
- Range max tracking
- Memoization vs bottom-up trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(a, K){
  const N=a.length, INF=1e18;
  const Kcap=Math.min(K,N);
  const dp=Array.from({length:N+1}, ()=>Array(Kcap+1).fill(INF));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=N;i++){
    for(let k=1;k<=Math.min(i,Kcap);k++){
      let maxV=0;
      for(let j=i-1;j>=0;j--){
        maxV = Math.max(maxV, a[j]);
        dp[i][k] = Math.min(dp[i][k], dp[j][k-1] + maxV);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[N][Kcap];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can you push to O(NK) with a monotone optimization?
- How would you adapt if batch cost were sum of maxes with a penalty per batch?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem assesses mastery of graph algorithms on DAGs, including topological sorting, feasibility verification under ordered constraints, and shortest-path computation.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort for DAGs
- DAG shortest-path DP (O(|V| + |E|))
- Feasibility checking with ordered mandatory edges
- Path reconstruction via predecessor pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestConstrainedPath(G, S, T, M) {
  const topo = topoSort(G);
  const idx = (v) => topo.indexOf(v);

  const canReach = (from, to) => {
    if (idx(from) >= idx(to)) return false;
    const reachable = new Set([from]);
    for (let i = idx(from); i < idx(to); i++) {
      for (const edge of G.adj[topo[i]]) {
        if (idx(edge.to) < idx(to)) reachable.add(edge.to);
      }
    }
    return reachable.has(to);
  };

  const points = [S, ...M.map(e => e[0]), ...M.map(e => e[1]), T];
  for (let i = 0; i < points.length - 1; i++) {
    if (!canReach(points[i], points[i + 1])) return null;
  }

  const segmentCost = (src, dst) => {
    const dist = {}; dist[src] = 0;
    for (let i = idx(src); i < idx(dst); i++) {
      const u = topo[i];
      for (const edge of G.adj[u]) {
        if (idx(edge.to) < idx(dst)) {
          if (dist[u] + edge.w < (dist[edge.to] ?? Infinity)) {
            dist[edge.to] = dist[u] + edge.w;
          }
        }
      }
    }
    return dist[dst];
  };

  let total = 0;
  const fullPath = [S];
  for (const [u, v, w] of M) {
    total += w;
    const seg = segmentCost(fullPath[fullPath.length - 1], u);
    if (seg === undefined) return null;
    total += seg;
  }
  const finalSeg = segmentCost(M[M.length - 1][1], T);
  if (finalSeg === undefined) return null;
  return total + finalSeg;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates dynamic programming skills with state expansion, requiring candidates to track whether the cell removal has been used. It tests proper handling of grid boundary conditions and provides opportunities to discuss space optimization strategies and compare memoization versus tabulation approaches.

## Key Concepts
- DP with auxiliary state tracking
